    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d350">

                <pb facs="0521" n="1985" sameAs="frus:frus1951v06p2/pg1985"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">689.90D/7–1151: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Pakistan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Warren</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Karachi</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-07-11T14:00:00+05:30">July 11, 1951—2 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>33. Be Deptel 11, July 6.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d350-f1"/> Pakistanis say there was nothing wrong with their
                    reception of Shah Wali, but that he refused all offers hospitality including
                    invitation to stay with Gov Gen,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d350-f2"/> and insisted on
                    haranguing protocol officer who met him on Pushtoonistan.</p>
                <p>Emb feels many of reported border incidents are not important in themselves and
                    that their chief significance lies in exaggerated publicity given to them by
                    each side as evidence of hostility and bad faith of the other. In our opinion a
                    conf to discuss prevention border raids such as has been suggested by Pakistanis
                    wld probably result in nothing more than an exchange of recriminations. What is
                    needed is not a method of preventing raids, but the will to stop them and to
                    stop talking about them.</p>
                <pb facs="0522" n="1986" xml:id="pg1986"/>
                <p>Consequently, even if Afghans reject a Pakistan counterproposal it seems to us
                    useful to call upon both govts to accept principle of point 2 our Nov 6
                    approach, namely to work toward preventing tribal incidents and avoiding
                    publicity on those that do occur.</p>
                <p>Dept pass London; sent Dept 33. Rptd info London 3, Kabul unnumbered.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Warren</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d350-f1" n="1" xml:id="d350-f1-content">
                <p>Same as telegram 8, to Kabul, July 6, <ref target="#pg1984">p. 1984</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d350-f2" n="2" xml:id="d350-f2-content">
                <p>Khwaja Nazimuddin, Governor General of Pakistan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
